  "result": "Done. All four tests in `tests/test_t1.py` pass.\n\n**What I implemented** in `catalog/service.py`:\n\n- `NotFound` exception and the `Catalog` class with the exact public interface from the spec.\n- Records live in a dict keyed by id, so `list` returns creation order for free and lookups are O(1).\n- `get`, `update`, and `list` return copies, so callers can't mutate internal state through the returned dicts.\n- `update` validates field names before touching the record, so an unknown field raises `ValueError` without partially applying valid ones.\n- Ids are never reused after deletion, so a stale id fails with `NotFound` rather than pointing at a newer record.\n\nI left the working tree uncommitted since you didn't ask for a commit.\n\n**Approach chosen:** Approach A, sequential integers. Ids are only ever minted inside `create` on a single in-process object, so the coordination-free generation that UUIDs offer has no use here, and the integer counter gives shorter, sortable, easier-to-debug ids.",
